# 2️⃣ Kafka vs RabbitMQ Backpressure

This is a **classic architecture comparison**.

---

## 🔹 Kafka Backpressure Model

Kafka is a **log-based streaming platform**.

### How Kafka handles pressure

* Writes go to disk
* Consumers pull at their own speed
* Messages accumulate safely

```Producer → Kafka (disk) → Consumer (slow)
```

### Characteristics

* Lag grows instead of blocking
* High throughput
* Designed for replay

### Pros

✅ Handles huge spikes
✅ Durable storage
✅ Reprocessing possible

### Cons

❌ Lag monitoring required
❌ Slow consumers can fall far behind

---

## 🔹 RabbitMQ Backpressure Model

RabbitMQ is a **queue-based messaging broker**.

### How RabbitMQ handles pressure

* Messages stay in memory
* If queues fill up → producers blocked

```Producer → Queue (memory) → Consumer
```

### Characteristics

* Push-based
* Immediate backpressure
* Limited retention

### Pros

✅ Fast delivery
✅ Natural backpressure
✅ Low latency

### Cons

❌ Not good for replay
❌ Memory pressure risks

---

## 🔹 Kafka vs RabbitMQ (Quick Table)

| Feature      | Kafka                | RabbitMQ        |
| ------------ | -------------------- | --------------- |
| Model        | Log-based            | Queue-based     |
| Storage      | Disk                 | Memory (mostly) |
| Backpressure | Lag-based            | Blocking        |
| Replay       | ✅ Yes                | ❌ No            |
| Throughput   | Very high            | Medium          |
| Best for     | Streaming, analytics | Task queues     |

